"Groups outcome-oriented initiatives by current focus, subsequent intent and longer-term options without inventing fixed delivery dates.\n\nUse when: Communicate product direction under substantial uncertainty. Revisit priorities as discovery changes the evidence.\n\nAvoid when: The audience needs contractual dates or a detailed resource-feasible schedule.\n\nVisual grammar: Three named horizons express relative planning confidence and focus. Initiative cards describe problems or outcomes rather than feature inventories. Status and evidence tags distinguish active work from options.\n\nBuild: State the product outcome and define what each horizon means. Place initiatives by focus, confidence and readiness. Review movement using learning and capacity rather than elapsed calendar time alone.\n\nCheck: Now contains work that is actually being addressed. Later items remain options unless explicitly committed. Horizon labels do not silently imply fixed calendar intervals.\n\nPitfalls: Treating the columns as disguised quarterly deadlines. Filling the roadmap with solution requests lacking a clear problem.\n\nExample: A membership service focuses now on renewal friction, next on family access and later on partnership opportunities as evidence develops.\n\nAlternatives: Milestone roadmap, Opportunity solution tree."
